{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527773291,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxMzM4MTcw"},"target":"9e3b120e80af19ead838cfd84c2f51ee4262954e5a699463f68be6f818cc876a","message":"More and more stuff is creeping into the code base that is concerned with setting up datasets for particular use cases. We have long identified the need to have some kind of dataset templates, to be able to do `datalad create --template howIneedittobe`. But no effort ever got there.\n\nSo let me propose something simple, with little required effort and high potential:\n\n- a template is a tarball (but see the end)\n- such tarball get's extracted at the location of a just created dataset (think `create_tree`). It is extracted after creation so the flags like `--force` still work as desired.\n- after extraction a Python script that is located in the root of the tarball (with a special name, maybe `.configure`) is executed, the sole argument being the absolute path of the dataset  and performs arbitrary (re-)configurations, additions, whatever\n- this script can use different interpreters (python for platform independence, bash for simplicity)\n- after the script is done the results are commited -- in practice this means we use `run` to perform this step\n- such template tarballs are looked up at a configurable set of locations (system, user)\n- a tarball doesn't actually need to be a tarball, but can also just be the configure\n\n- `create` should be able to execute multiple templates sequentially, so we can orthogonalize the scenarios we want to support.\n\nafterwards anybody can come up with and share there preferred dataset setups.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527773496,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxMzM5NTUx"},"target":"9e3b120e80af19ead838cfd84c2f51ee4262954e5a699463f68be6f818cc876a","message":"More and more stuff is creeping into the code base that is concerned with setting up datasets for particular use cases. We have long identified the need to have some kind of dataset templates, to be able to do `datalad create --template howIneedittobe`. But no effort ever got there.\n\nSo let me propose something simple, with little required effort and high potential:\n\n- a template is a tarball (but see the end)\n- such tarball get's extracted at the location of a just created dataset (think `create_tree`). It is extracted after creation so the flags like `--force` still work as desired.\n- after extraction a Python script that is located in the root of the tarball (with a special name, maybe `.configure`) is executed, the sole argument being the absolute path of the dataset  and performs arbitrary (re-)configurations, additions, whatever\n- this script can use different interpreters (python for platform independence, bash for simplicity)\n- after the script is done the results are commited -- in practice this means we use `run` to perform this step\n- such template tarballs are looked up at a configurable set of locations (system, user)\n- a tarball doesn't actually need to be a tarball, but can also just be the configure\n\n- `create` should be able to execute multiple templates sequentially, so we can orthogonalize the scenarios we want to support.\n\nafterwards anybody can come up with and share there preferred dataset setups.\n\n### candidates for API removal after this works:\n\n- `create(native_metadata)`\n- `create(text_no_annex)`\n\n### other related aspects\n- entire business of inheriting configuration from a parent dataset","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527773565,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxMzM5OTYz"},"target":"9e3b120e80af19ead838cfd84c2f51ee4262954e5a699463f68be6f818cc876a","message":"More and more stuff is creeping into the code base that is concerned with setting up datasets for particular use cases. We have long identified the need to have some kind of dataset templates, to be able to do `datalad create --template howIneedittobe`. But no effort ever got there.\n\nSo let me propose something simple, with little required effort and high potential:\n\n- a template is a tarball (but see the end)\n- such tarball get's extracted at the location of a just created dataset (think `create_tree`). It is extracted after creation so the flags like `--force` still work as desired.\n- after extraction a Python script that is located in the root of the tarball (with a special name, maybe `.configure`) is executed, the sole argument being the absolute path of the dataset  and performs arbitrary (re-)configurations, additions, whatever\n- this script can use different interpreters (python for platform independence, bash for simplicity)\n- after the script is done the results are commited -- in practice this means we use `run` to perform this step\n- such template tarballs are looked up at a configurable set of locations (system, user)\n- a tarball doesn't actually need to be a tarball, but can also just be the configure\n\n- `create` should be able to execute multiple templates sequentially, so we can orthogonalize the scenarios we want to support.\n\nafterwards anybody can come up with and share there preferred dataset setups.\n\n### candidates for API removal after this works:\n\n- `create(native_metadata)`\n- `create(text_no_annex)`\n\n### other related aspects\n- entire business of inheriting configuration from a parent dataset","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527773912,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxMzQyMjIx"},"target":"9e3b120e80af19ead838cfd84c2f51ee4262954e5a699463f68be6f818cc876a","message":"More and more stuff is creeping into the code base that is concerned with setting up datasets for particular use cases. We have long identified the need to have some kind of dataset templates, to be able to do `datalad create --template howIneedittobe`. But no effort ever got there.\n\nSo let me propose something simple, with little required effort and high potential:\n\n- a template is a tarball (but see the end)\n- such tarball get's extracted at the location of a just created dataset (think `create_tree`). It is extracted after creation so the flags like `--force` still work as desired.\n- after extraction a Python script that is located in the root of the tarball (with a special name, maybe `.configure`) is executed, the sole argument being the absolute path of the dataset  and performs arbitrary (re-)configurations, additions, whatever\n- this script can use different interpreters (python for platform independence, bash for simplicity)\n- after the script is done the results are commited -- in practice this means we use `run` to perform this step\n- such template tarballs are looked up at a configurable set of locations (system, user)\n- a tarball doesn't actually need to be a tarball, but can also just be the configure\n\n- `create` should be able to execute multiple templates sequentially, so we can orthogonalize the scenarios we want to support.\n\nafterwards anybody can come up with and share there preferred dataset setups.\n\n### candidates for API removal after this works:\n\n- `create(native_metadata)`\n- `create(text_no_annex)`\n\n### other related aspects\n- entire business of inheriting configuration from a parent dataset\n- this is basically an interface for canned procedures. Configuration of datasets is just one flavor of where this functionality is useful. I think what this will lead to is the return of the `run_before/after` functionality that we briefly enjoyed in the API. This time however, there is no need to go through plugins or something as complicated, because we have `run` now. This will enable us to even implement \"canned procedures\" that require input arguments.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527774898,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxMzQ5MzQx"},"target":"9e3b120e80af19ead838cfd84c2f51ee4262954e5a699463f68be6f818cc876a","message":"More and more stuff is creeping into the code base that is concerned with setting up datasets for particular use cases. We have long identified the need to have some kind of dataset templates, to be able to do `datalad create --template howIneedittobe`. But no effort ever got there.\n\nSo let me propose something simple, with little required effort and high potential:\n\n- a template is a tarball (but see the end)\n- such tarball get's extracted at the location of a just created dataset (think `create_tree`). It is extracted after creation so the flags like `--force` still work as desired.\n- after extraction a Python script that is located in the root of the tarball (with a special name, maybe `.configure`) is executed, the sole argument being the absolute path of the dataset  and performs arbitrary (re-)configurations, additions, whatever\n- this script can use different interpreters (python for platform independence, bash for simplicity)\n- after the script is done the results are commited -- in practice this means we use `run` to perform this step\n- such template tarballs are looked up at a configurable set of locations (system, user)\n- a tarball doesn't actually need to be a tarball, but can also just be the configure\n\n- `create` should be able to execute multiple templates sequentially, so we can orthogonalize the scenarios we want to support.\n\nafterwards anybody can come up with and share there preferred dataset setups.\n\n### candidates for API removal after this works:\n\n- `create(native_metadata)`\n- `create(text_no_annex)`\n\n### other related aspects\n- entire business of inheriting configuration from a parent dataset\n- this is basically an interface for canned procedures. Configuration of datasets is just one flavor of where this functionality is useful. I think what this will lead to is the return of the `run_before/after` functionality that we briefly enjoyed in the API. This time however, there is no need to go through plugins or something as complicated, because we have `run` now. This will enable us to even implement \"canned procedures\" that require input arguments.\n\n### conclusion\n\nSo we end up with a new command `run-procedure` that sorts out, how such a script needs to be executed, and tailored a call to `run` to execute it (analog to `containers-run` but without containtainers). That means we can support extensions of datalad functionality that do not require going through the Python API.\n\nOne aspect would need a further extension of the `run` API: ATM we require a clean dataset before `run` does anything. But a simple `add` can work with a dirty dataset just fine. Nobody says that a canned procedure must not create datalad commands directly and must rely on `run` to capture all the changes. So we either need to extend the `run` API to support all the running aspects without the result capture (i.e. a mere frontend for the `Runner` class). Or we need to expose and reuse the pieces of `run` that do that and also use them for `run-procedure`. I'd prefer to first solution, as the handling of `--inputs` and `--outputs` is already *very* convenient.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527774917,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxMzQ5NDk4"},"target":"9e3b120e80af19ead838cfd84c2f51ee4262954e5a699463f68be6f818cc876a","message":"More and more stuff is creeping into the code base that is concerned with setting up datasets for particular use cases. We have long identified the need to have some kind of dataset templates, to be able to do `datalad create --template howIneedittobe`. But no effort ever got there.\n\nSo let me propose something simple, with little required effort and high potential:\n\n- a template is a tarball (but see the end)\n- such tarball get's extracted at the location of a just created dataset (think `create_tree`). It is extracted after creation so the flags like `--force` still work as desired.\n- after extraction a Python script that is located in the root of the tarball (with a special name, maybe `.configure`) is executed, the sole argument being the absolute path of the dataset  and performs arbitrary (re-)configurations, additions, whatever\n- this script can use different interpreters (python for platform independence, bash for simplicity)\n- after the script is done the results are commited -- in practice this means we use `run` to perform this step\n- such template tarballs are looked up at a configurable set of locations (system, user)\n- a tarball doesn't actually need to be a tarball, but can also just be the configure\n\n- `create` should be able to execute multiple templates sequentially, so we can orthogonalize the scenarios we want to support.\n\nafterwards anybody can come up with and share there preferred dataset setups.\n\n### candidates for API removal after this works:\n\n- `create(native_metadata)`\n- `create(text_no_annex)`\n\n### other related aspects\n- entire business of inheriting configuration from a parent dataset\n- this is basically an interface for canned procedures. Configuration of datasets is just one flavor of where this functionality is useful. I think what this will lead to is the return of the `run_before/after` functionality that we briefly enjoyed in the API. This time however, there is no need to go through plugins or something as complicated, because we have `run` now. This will enable us to even implement \"canned procedures\" that require input arguments.\n\n### conclusion\n\nSo we end up with a new command `run-procedure` that sorts out, how such a script needs to be executed, and tailored a call to `run` to execute it (analog to `containers-run` but without containtainers). That means we can support extensions of datalad functionality that do not require going through the Python API.\n\nOne aspect would need a further extension of the `run` API: ATM we require a clean dataset before `run` does anything. But a simple `add` can work with a dirty dataset just fine. Nobody says that a canned procedure must not create datalad commands directly and must rely on `run` to capture all the changes. So we either need to extend the `run` API to support all the running aspects without the result capture (i.e. a mere frontend for the `Runner` class). Or we need to expose and reuse the pieces of `run` that do that and also use them for `run-procedure`. I'd prefer to first solution, as the handling of `--inputs` and `--outputs` is already *very* convenient.\n\n@kyleam what do you think?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527774999,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxMzUwMDkx"},"target":"9e3b120e80af19ead838cfd84c2f51ee4262954e5a699463f68be6f818cc876a","message":"More and more stuff is creeping into the code base that is concerned with setting up datasets for particular use cases. We have long identified the need to have some kind of dataset templates, to be able to do `datalad create --template howIneedittobe`. But no effort ever got there.\n\nSo let me propose something simple, with little required effort and high potential:\n\n- a template is a tarball (but see the end)\n- such tarball get's extracted at the location of a just created dataset (think `create_tree`). It is extracted after creation so the flags like `--force` still work as desired.\n- after extraction a Python script that is located in the root of the tarball (with a special name, maybe `.configure`) is executed, the sole argument being the absolute path of the dataset  and performs arbitrary (re-)configurations, additions, whatever\n- this script can use different interpreters (python for platform independence, bash for simplicity)\n- after the script is done the results are commited -- in practice this means we use `run` to perform this step\n- such template tarballs are looked up at a configurable set of locations (system, user)\n- a tarball doesn't actually need to be a tarball, but can also just be the configure\n\n- `create` should be able to execute multiple templates sequentially, so we can orthogonalize the scenarios we want to support.\n\nafterwards anybody can come up with and share there preferred dataset setups.\n\n### candidates for API removal after this works:\n\n- `create(native_metadata)`\n- `create(text_no_annex)`\n- completely discontinue the Python-only plugin interface code and implementations\n\n### other related aspects\n- entire business of inheriting configuration from a parent dataset\n- this is basically an interface for canned procedures. Configuration of datasets is just one flavor of where this functionality is useful. I think what this will lead to is the return of the `run_before/after` functionality that we briefly enjoyed in the API. This time however, there is no need to go through plugins or something as complicated, because we have `run` now. This will enable us to even implement \"canned procedures\" that require input arguments.\n\n### conclusion\n\nSo we end up with a new command `run-procedure` that sorts out, how such a script needs to be executed, and tailored a call to `run` to execute it (analog to `containers-run` but without containtainers). That means we can support extensions of datalad functionality that do not require going through the Python API.\n\nOne aspect would need a further extension of the `run` API: ATM we require a clean dataset before `run` does anything. But a simple `add` can work with a dirty dataset just fine. Nobody says that a canned procedure must not create datalad commands directly and must rely on `run` to capture all the changes. So we either need to extend the `run` API to support all the running aspects without the result capture (i.e. a mere frontend for the `Runner` class). Or we need to expose and reuse the pieces of `run` that do that and also use them for `run-procedure`. I'd prefer to first solution, as the handling of `--inputs` and `--outputs` is already *very* convenient.\n\n@kyleam what do you think?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527775037,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxMzUwMzc2"},"target":"9e3b120e80af19ead838cfd84c2f51ee4262954e5a699463f68be6f818cc876a","message":"More and more stuff is creeping into the code base that is concerned with setting up datasets for particular use cases. We have long identified the need to have some kind of dataset templates, to be able to do `datalad create --template howIneedittobe`. But no effort ever got there.\n\nSo let me propose something simple, with little required effort and high potential:\n\n- a template is a tarball (but see the end)\n- such tarball get's extracted at the location of a just created dataset (think `create_tree`). It is extracted after creation so the flags like `--force` still work as desired.\n- after extraction a Python script that is located in the root of the tarball (with a special name, maybe `.configure`) is executed, the sole argument being the absolute path of the dataset  and performs arbitrary (re-)configurations, additions, whatever\n- this script can use different interpreters (python for platform independence, bash for simplicity)\n- after the script is done the results are commited -- in practice this means we use `run` to perform this step\n- such template tarballs are looked up at a configurable set of locations (system, user)\n- a tarball doesn't actually need to be a tarball, but can also just be the configure\n\n- `create` should be able to execute multiple templates sequentially, so we can orthogonalize the scenarios we want to support.\n\nafterwards anybody can come up with and share there preferred dataset setups.\n\n### candidates for API removal after this works:\n\n- `create(native_metadata)`\n- `create(text_no_annex)`\n- completely discontinue the Python-only plugin interface code and implementations: either proper commands or this new one for any given functionality\n\n### other related aspects\n- entire business of inheriting configuration from a parent dataset\n- this is basically an interface for canned procedures. Configuration of datasets is just one flavor of where this functionality is useful. I think what this will lead to is the return of the `run_before/after` functionality that we briefly enjoyed in the API. This time however, there is no need to go through plugins or something as complicated, because we have `run` now. This will enable us to even implement \"canned procedures\" that require input arguments.\n\n### conclusion\n\nSo we end up with a new command `run-procedure` that sorts out, how such a script needs to be executed, and tailored a call to `run` to execute it (analog to `containers-run` but without containtainers). That means we can support extensions of datalad functionality that do not require going through the Python API.\n\nOne aspect would need a further extension of the `run` API: ATM we require a clean dataset before `run` does anything. But a simple `add` can work with a dirty dataset just fine. Nobody says that a canned procedure must not create datalad commands directly and must rely on `run` to capture all the changes. So we either need to extend the `run` API to support all the running aspects without the result capture (i.e. a mere frontend for the `Runner` class). Or we need to expose and reuse the pieces of `run` that do that and also use them for `run-procedure`. I'd prefer to first solution, as the handling of `--inputs` and `--outputs` is already *very* convenient.\n\n@kyleam what do you think?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527775067,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxMzUwNjMx"},"target":"9e3b120e80af19ead838cfd84c2f51ee4262954e5a699463f68be6f818cc876a","message":"More and more stuff is creeping into the code base that is concerned with setting up datasets for particular use cases. We have long identified the need to have some kind of dataset templates, to be able to do `datalad create --template howIneedittobe`. But no effort ever got there.\n\nSo let me propose something simple, with little required effort and high potential:\n\n- a template is a tarball (but see the end)\n- such tarball get's extracted at the location of a just created dataset (think `create_tree`). It is extracted after creation so the flags like `--force` still work as desired.\n- after extraction a Python script that is located in the root of the tarball (with a special name, maybe `.configure`) is executed, the sole argument being the absolute path of the dataset  and performs arbitrary (re-)configurations, additions, whatever\n- this script can use different interpreters (python for platform independence, bash for simplicity)\n- after the script is done the results are commited -- in practice this means we use `run` to perform this step\n- such template tarballs are looked up at a configurable set of locations (system, user)\n- a tarball doesn't actually need to be a tarball, but can also just be the configure\n\n- `create` should be able to execute multiple templates sequentially, so we can orthogonalize the scenarios we want to support.\n\nafterwards anybody can come up with and share there preferred dataset setups.\n\n### candidates for API removal after this works:\n\n- `create(native_metadata)`\n- `create(text_no_annex)`\n- completely discontinue the Python-only plugin interface code and implementations: either proper commands or this new one for any given functionality\n\n### other related aspects\n- entire business of inheriting configuration from a parent dataset\n- this is basically an interface for canned procedures. Configuration of datasets is just one flavor of where this functionality is useful. I think what this will lead to is the return of the `--run-before/after` functionality that we briefly enjoyed in the API. This time however, there is no need to go through plugins or something as complicated, because we have `run` now. This will enable us to even implement \"canned procedures\" that require input arguments.\n\n### conclusion\n\nSo we end up with a new command `run-procedure` that sorts out, how such a script needs to be executed, and tailored a call to `run` to execute it (analog to `containers-run` but without containtainers). That means we can support extensions of datalad functionality that do not require going through the Python API.\n\nOne aspect would need a further extension of the `run` API: ATM we require a clean dataset before `run` does anything. But a simple `add` can work with a dirty dataset just fine. Nobody says that a canned procedure must not create datalad commands directly and must rely on `run` to capture all the changes. So we either need to extend the `run` API to support all the running aspects without the result capture (i.e. a mere frontend for the `Runner` class). Or we need to expose and reuse the pieces of `run` that do that and also use them for `run-procedure`. I'd prefer to first solution, as the handling of `--inputs` and `--outputs` is already *very* convenient.\n\n@kyleam what do you think?","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527753609,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE2NTUzNjY5ODU="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527753609,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE2NTUzNjY5ODc="},"added":["feedback-desired"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1527754563,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MzQ1MDM5MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2593#issuecomment-393450390"},"message":"Sounds pretty flexible to me. And an approach like sth similar to what I started with new testrepos could  easily be added later on and serve as something that just makes the `.configure` scripts more clean and convenient, particularly wrt to using finegrained `datalad/annex/git` commands to set up a nice history (not just one big fat `datalad-run` commit).\n\nSo: I like it as low-effort, high-impact solution that is sufficiently general an approach, that there's no need to actually replace it later on, but just enhance it with an actually independent layer.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1527754630,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MzQ1MDczMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2593#issuecomment-393450732"},"message":"And FTR: tarball (or any kind of archive) should be an option indeed.","files":null},{"type":2,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527774932,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50MTY1NjA1MDEyNg=="},"title":"Urgent: dataset templates -\u003e `run-procedure` command","was":"Urgent: `run-procedure` command"},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1527803086,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MzY5MDU4MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2593#issuecomment-393690581"},"message":"I'm still digesting this, especially since I'm not really familiar with the previous thoughts/attempts in this direction, but it sounds like a good approach.\n\n\u003e So we either need to extend the run API to support all the running aspects without the result capture (i.e. a mere frontend for the Runner class). Or we need to expose and reuse the pieces of run that do that and also use them for run-procedure. I'd prefer to first solution\n\nIIUC the first option seems OK to me -- essentially a flag that says \"don't complain if the repo is dirty and don't save\", right?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527833792,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5Mzc3MjU5Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2593#issuecomment-393772597"},"message":"@kyleam yes, exactly. Don't complain, don't save, leave everything to the commands you are running.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576937626,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2ODE4Mzk4Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2596#issuecomment-568183987"},"message":"Closing FOI","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576937626,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjkwMjcxMzQwNg=="},"status":2}]}